  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/471A14">Remark <span data-tag="471A14">5.5.8.20</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be an $\infty $-category which admits small colimits, and let $X$ be an object of $\calC $. Then $X$ is compact and projective if and only if $X$ corepresents a functor $\calC \rightarrow \sSet $ which preserves sifted colimits. The “only if” direction is obvious, and the converse follows from Corollary <a href="/tag/A9C1DB" data-tag="A9C1DB">5.5.8.17</a>. </p>
